> Well, reflection reflects rays/beams/breaths,

It only reflects rays (including breaths), not beams -- at least given
the definition of "beam" in a NetHack context that I'm aware of:
a directional effect that doesn't bounce and isn't reflected.
Rays, on the other hand, bounce and can be reflected; they're also
displayed on the map somehow.

(I'd say beams always hit, but force bolt is an exception there.
Rays have a chance of missing, though.)

Force bolt, teleportation and polymorph are examples of beams;
magic missile, dragon breaths and death rays are examples of rays.

> and magic resistance provides
> resistance against certain magic effects. - Why do you think they have in
> some way commonalities?

There /are/ a few things where having only one of magic resistance/
reflection is enough, admittedly: for example, magic missiles
or death rays.

> Typically you want to get both. [...] Reflection protects
> also from the gaze attack of floating eyes, and (as long as you are not
> invisible) also from Medusa's stoning gaze.

Reflection even protects from Medusa's gaze if you're invisible.
It just means Medusa will not notice her reflection, so she will not
be turned to stone instantly as she otherwise would.

